To debug a Service Bus triggered Azure Function locally using Postman, you can temporarily convert it to an HTTP trigger, send a mock payload from Postman, and test the logic without depending on the actual Service Bus.


---

✅ Why Do This?

Since Service Bus (especially private) may not be reachable from your local machine, converting to an HTTP trigger allows you to:

Easily simulate messages

Use Postman or curl for testing

Step through the logic with breakpoints in Visual Studio



---

🔄 Step-by-Step: Convert Service Bus Trigger to HTTP Trigger

🔹 Original Service Bus Trigger Function

[FunctionName("ProcessOrder")]
public void Run(
    [ServiceBusTrigger("orderqueue", Connection = "ServiceBusConnection")] 
    string message, 
    ILogger log)
{
    log.LogInformation($"Received: {message}");
    // Your processing logic
}

🔹 Temporary HTTP Trigger Version for Local Debugging

[FunctionName("ProcessOrderHttp")]
public IActionResult RunHttp(
    [HttpTrigger(AuthorizationLevel.Anonymous, "post", Route = null)] 
    HttpRequest req, 
    ILogger log)
{
    using var reader = new StreamReader(req.Body);
    string message = reader.ReadToEnd();

    log.LogInformation($"Simulated message: {message}");

    // Call your business logic here
    // e.g., ProcessOrder(message);

    return new OkObjectResult("Processed message successfully");
}


---

🧪 Test in Postman

1. Run the function in Visual Studio (F5)


2. The console will show a local URL like:

http://localhost:7071/api/ProcessOrderHttp


3. In Postman:

Method: POST

URL: http://localhost:7071/api/ProcessOrderHttp

Body: Raw → JSON (or plain text if needed)




Example JSON:

{
  "orderId": "1234",
  "customer": "Pankaj",
  "items": ["apple", "banana"]
}

Or just a plain text message:

Test message from Postman


---

🔁 Once Done: Revert Back to Service Bus Trigger

After you're happy with your logic, revert the function to use the original Service Bus trigger.

You can even keep both versions if you want to maintain an easy debug path:

#if DEBUG
    [FunctionName("ProcessOrderHttp")]
    public IActionResult RunHttp(...) { ... }
#else
    [FunctionName("ProcessOrder")]
    public void Run(...) { ... }
#endif


---

📌 Tip

Extract your business logic into a separate method:

void ProcessOrder(string message) { ... }

This way, both your HTTP and Service Bus versions can call the same method for consistent behavior.


